# Line endings, declared rather than inherited.
#
# Before this file the repository had none, and every line ending in a working
# tree came from whatever `core.autocrlf` the machine happened to carry. Here
# it was `true`, set at system level by the Git for Windows installer, so it is
# neither in .git/config nor in the user's global config and does not travel
# with a clone. The index was already LF throughout; the working tree was 177
# files CRLF, 67 LF and 4 with BOTH inside one file.
#
# That combination produced a specific and repeatable trap. A tool that
# rewrites a file with LF endings -- `sed -i`, a Python text-mode write, any
# heredoc -- changes every line in the working tree while `git diff` shows only
# the line the edit meant to change, because the diff is taken against a
# normalised blob. The change is real, invisible, and lands in whatever the
# next tool reads. It cost time in P8, in P10 and twice in P11.
#
# `eol=lf` ends it by making LF correct everywhere: index, working tree, and
# whatever a tool writes back. Nothing in this repository reads a line ending
# -- `grep -rnF '\r\n'` over src/, tools/ and tests/ finds three comments
# describing this hazard and no code implementing anything against it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Two ASCII text files carried a lone CR each, inside a sentence that meant to
# print the escape sequence \r\n and printed the bytes instead. Git's binary
# heuristic reads a lone CR as binary, so both were classified `-text`: no
# diff, no merge, no normalisation. The bytes are repaired in the same commit
# as this file; these entries mean a future one is diagnosed rather than
# silently reclassified.
*.md    text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf

# Declared binary. Without this git guesses from content, and it had already
# guessed wrong twice above.
*.docx  binary
*.pdf   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
